<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">East Karelia</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Russian Karelia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">East <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karelia">Karelia</a> is the part of Karelia that lay under Russian rule, east of the Finnish border, between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-ladoga">Lake Ladoga</a>, Lake Onega and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/white-sea">White Sea</a>. Much of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalevala">Kalevala</a> folk poetry was collected among its Karelian villages. After 1917 Finnish nationalists sought to unite it with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a>, leading to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uhtua">Uhtua</a> republic and other expeditions, but it remained Soviet.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/east-karelia.jpg" alt="East Karelia" width="320" height="498" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: User Jniemenmaa on en.wikipedia · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:East_and_West_Karelias.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
